{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/1476140-aberrant-dots-eh","id":1476140,"title":"Aberrant - Dots + Eh?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","The Trinity Continuum","Trinity Continuum: Aberrant"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-03-09T20:08:15-08:00","id":1476140,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon2.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> Aberrant - Dots + Eh?","content":"So, I was comparing and contrasting the Teleportation Aptitude of the Upeo Wa Macho with their analogue Quantum Powers in the Aberrant Core Book and I encountered a rules notation that leaves me somewhat confused.<br /> <br /> The &quot;Dots + X or Dots - X or X or Dots&quot; in the Quantum Minimum explanation is a bit confusing. So, please, help me if you can:<br /> <br /> Stewie the Wonderful Aberrant has {Random Power A} and that Powers' Quantum Minimum is Quantum Dots + 2. Stewie has (what I often call) A Base Quantum score of 3 (I tend to refer to the main Quantum Trait as &quot;Base&quot; Quantum to differentiate between it and the &quot;spendable&quot; Quantum the character uses). So, his equation for said power is 3 + 2.<br /> <br /> What does that mean? What would it mean if it was 3 - 2? Or 3? Or 2?<br /> <br /> Please explain this to me like I am seven years old. Use blocks, stick-tape, lego, construction paper, paste, crayons...whatever you need....","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/21614-corradus","name":"Corradus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar21614_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"18"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-09T20:33:21-08:00","id":1476143,"title":"","content":"If you're a Teleporter, a Quantum Minimum of Dots + 1 means that, however many dots you have in the power, you need a Quantum score one higher. So Teleport 1 requires Quantum 2 while Teleport 4 requires Quantum 5.<br /> <br /> If you're a Warper, a Quantum Minimum of Dots + 2 means that, however many dots you have in the power, you need a Quantum score two higher. So Teleport 1 requires Quantum 3 while Teleport 3 requires Quantum 5.<br /> <br /> This is why characters with Warp probably shouldn't bother taking the Impose Tag. Even if you're allowed to start the game with Quantum 5 (which isn't guaranteed), you've got a max base Q+P dice pool of 8, versus a difficulty of Defense + 1. The weakest enemy in the book has Defense 3 without taking a defensive action, meaning you have to get at least 4 successes while rolling 8 dice. The Core book's &quot;Minor Threat&quot; entry has Defense 3. In fact, the only enemies you're reasonably able to take out are Minor Characters with a Primary Pool of 4, since that gives them a Defense of 1, and they never take defensive actions.<br /> <br /> edit:<br /> <br /> If you have Morph, which has a Quantum Minimum of Dots - 3, this means a Nova with Morph 4 only requires 4-3 = Quantum 1, while Morph 5 requires 5-3 = Quantum 2. You obviously always need at least Quantum 1.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25506,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25506-shepherdboi","user":"Shepherdboi","date":". 03-09-2022, 11:39 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25506-shepherdboi","name":"Shepherdboi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25506_1.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"364"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-10T20:56:42-08:00","id":1476189,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shepherdboi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/1476140-aberrant-dots-eh?p=1476143#post1476143\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is why characters with Warp probably shouldn't bother taking the Impose Tag. Even if you're allowed to start the game with Quantum 5 (which isn't guaranteed), you've got a max base Q+P dice pool of 8, versus a difficulty of Defense + 1. The weakest enemy in the book has Defense 3 without taking a defensive action, meaning you have to get at least 4 successes while rolling 8 dice. The Core book's &quot;Minor Threat&quot; entry has Defense 3. In fact, the only enemies you're reasonably able to take out are Minor Characters with a Primary Pool of 4, since that gives them a Defense of 1, and they never take defensive actions.</div> </div> </div> </div>We haven't played with that power yet, but I can see it coming up as my boys like Generator Rex and I can totally see one of them playing Breach or someone like her.<br /> <br /> I think I can understand the motivation behind the need for Warp's high Defense roll requirements (why bother attacking someone when you can Warp them kilometres away... <i>or straight up</i>?). That said, I think they've gone a little too far the other way. As you state, you need four successes to Warp even the lowliest of security guards away, and that's generally not possible without spending Momentum (&amp; a good amount of it). Instead of discouraging it's use against everyone, they've made it impossible to use against <i><b>anyone</b></i>. Not sure I like that.<br /> <br /> If you're going to require more successes, there should be a costly (but manageable) way of getting an Enhancement to the roll. For example, Morph allows you to spend 2 Quantum Points for a +1 Enhancement (up to a max of 6 Quantum Points / +3 Enhancement). I can see why they did that - copying someone's features exactly requires 4 successes. Sound like familiar math to anyone else? Seems like a reasonable rule for this power when using Impose to me.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26905-eternl-knight","name":"Eternl Knight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26905_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"106"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-11T05:14:05-08:00","id":1476203,"title":"","content":"That's where splitting antagonist Defense scores between actual Defense and Soft Armor is useful. I usually do 50/50, depending on who they are.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8719-florin","name":"Florin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"940"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-11T14:09:04-08:00","id":1476224,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Eternl Knight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/1476140-aberrant-dots-eh?p=1476189#post1476189\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think I can understand the motivation behind the need for Warp's high Defense roll requirements (why bother attacking someone when you can Warp them kilometres away... <i>or straight up</i>?). That said, I think they've gone a little too far the other way. As you state, you need four successes to Warp even the lowliest of security guards away, and that's generally not possible without spending Momentum (&amp; a good amount of it). Instead of discouraging it's use against everyone, they've made it impossible to use against <i><b>anyone</b></i>. Not sure I like that.</div> </div> </div> </div>Even more annoying, Teleport offers the same use of the Impose Tag, except the Difficulty is your opponent's Defense instead of Defense+1, and your Q+P pool can go to 9 dice, so a Teleporter gets to roll more dice versus a lower difficulty to accomplish the same thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25506-shepherdboi","name":"Shepherdboi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25506_1.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"364"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-11T17:21:01-08:00","id":1476243,"title":"","content":"Well yes, because with Teleport you\u2019re directly shifting a target\u2019s location, with Warp you\u2019re hoping they fall into a hole.<br /> <br /> Warp is good for many things, but being an attacking power isn\u2019t one of them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Writer. Developer.</i> <b>World of Darkness</b> | <b>Chronicles of Darkness</b> | <b>The Trinity Continuum</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/156-bunyip","name":"Bunyip","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1880"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-13T21:10:22-07:00","id":1476358,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bunyip</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/1476140-aberrant-dots-eh?p=1476243#post1476243\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well yes, because with Teleport you\u2019re directly shifting a target\u2019s location, with Warp you\u2019re hoping they fall into a hole.<br /> <br /> Warp is good for many things, but being an attacking power isn\u2019t one of them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, according to the power description, they're not <i>falling into a hole</i> - the nova is making the portal <i>engulf a target</i>. Not trying to be a pain here, but if we're justifying how the powers work - going by their description in the book seems reasonable. The book sounds very much like how Breach from Generator Rex uses portals as attacks - the portals move slightly from one point to another engulfing the target. \ud83e\udd37<br /> <br /> Still, that's &quot;real world logic&quot;. Not &quot;super hero logic&quot; or &quot;game logic&quot;. It especially doesn't make sense when you take into account <i>Power Stunts</i>. Say you have a starting character who has the power <b>Elemental Mastery (Space)</b>. In Close range combat, instead of using their space-tearing <b>Quantum Attack</b> that might obliterate the non-malicious security guard just doing their job - they decide to blip them somewhere else safe but out of the way. Now, no matter whether your describe the attack as them being engulfed in a shimmering space portal or them <i>bamfing</i> away in a cloud of blue space-smoke - the effect is the same: Close range attack, rolling <b>Quantum</b> + <b>Elemental Mastery</b> dice, any successes send them somewhere else in range of your power. However, depending on whether the shimmering portal description is considered <b>Warp</b> or <b>Teleport</b>, the mechanical die roll results required to succeed are different for <i>purely narrative reasons</i> (and, at build time, harder to justify the Impose purchase at all when a <b>Teleport</b> power stunt is an option).<br /> <br /> I can see setting the difficulty at <i>&quot;Defense +1 Success per Range band beyond Close&quot;</i> to represent trying to create the portal at the right spot further &amp; further away from the nova. That has narrative &amp; game logic to it. Harder to accurately quickly put a portal in the eact right location at the exact right second at range. <b>Teleport</b> only offers a Close range option requiring you to touch the target*, the <b>Warp</b> power gives you a ranged version <u>but</u> at increasing difficulty the further out you are.<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:10px\"><i>* On this point, touching a target seems to be more a Dexterity+Close Combat or Dexterity+Athletics roll more than a power roll, kind of like Quantum Attack, but that's just me.</i></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26905,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26905-eternl-knight","user":"Eternl Knight","date":". 03-14-2022, 12:13 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26905-eternl-knight","name":"Eternl Knight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26905_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"106"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}